"""King Arthur and the Round Table: a VenvLearning retelling for Grade 4. Chapters 7–12."""
from readers.arthur_a import ch

ch("Sir Tristram",
   [("tribute", "money or goods one ruler is forced to pay another"), ("harp", "a stringed instrument played by plucking"),
    ("potion", "a drink with magic or medicine in it"), ("torn", "pulled between two choices")],
   ["Far to the west, in the land of Cornwall, ruled a king named Mark. Every year, King Mark had to send a tribute of gold to the King of Ireland. One year, the Irish king sent his champion, a giant knight named Sir Marhaus, to collect it. “Pay,” said Marhaus, “or send a knight to fight me. If your knight loses, you must pay forever.”",
    "No knight in Cornwall dared to fight Marhaus. Then King Mark's young nephew, Tristram, stepped forward. Tristram was a skilled hunter and a wonderful harp player, but he was barely a knight. “I will fight him,” he said.",
    "Tristram and Marhaus fought on a little island in the sea. It was a terrible battle, but at last Tristram won, and Marhaus fled back to Ireland, where he died of his wounds. Cornwall was free of the tribute. But Tristram had been wounded by Marhaus's spear, and his wound would not heal.",
    "Someone told him that only in Ireland could he be cured. So Tristram disguised himself, called himself Tramtrist, and sailed there with his harp. In Ireland, the king's daughter, the beautiful Princess Isoude, was famous for her skill in healing. She cared for Tristram and made him well. In return, he taught her to play the harp. They became close friends. But Tristram had to leave in a hurry when the Irish queen discovered he was the knight who had killed her brother, Marhaus.",
    "Back in Cornwall, Tristram told King Mark about the lovely princess. King Mark decided that he would marry Isoude himself, and he sent Tristram to Ireland to bring her back. Tristram was loyal to his uncle, so he went, even though his heart was heavy.",
    "Isoude's mother wanted her daughter to be happy in her marriage. She made a magic potion and gave it to Isoude's maid. “Give this to Isoude and King Mark on their wedding day,” she said, “and they will love each other forever.”",
    "But on the ship to Cornwall, the day was hot. Tristram and Isoude were thirsty, and they found the bottle. Thinking it was ordinary wine, they shared it. From that moment, they loved each other with a love that could never end.",
    "Isoude married King Mark as she had promised, but she and Tristram could not stop loving each other. Tristram was torn. He wanted to be loyal to his uncle, the king, but he also loved Isoude with his whole heart. King Mark grew jealous and angry, and at last he banished Tristram from Cornwall.",
    "Tristram wandered far and wide. He came to King Arthur's court, where he did so many brave deeds that he was given a seat at the Round Table. People said that only Sir Lancelot was a better knight. The two great knights became good friends.",
    "But no matter where Tristram went or how much glory he won, he never forgot Isoude, and he was never truly happy again. The story of Tristram reminded the knights of the Round Table that even the bravest person can be caught between two loyalties, and that a choice made in a single moment can shape an entire life."])

ch("The End of Merlin",
   [("foresee", "to know about something before it happens"), ("fate", "what is going to happen; destiny"),
    ("apprentice", "a person who learns a skill from an expert"), ("enchant", "to put under a magic spell")],
   ["Merlin had helped King Arthur in many ways. He had protected Arthur as a baby, led him to Excalibur, and given him wise advice. But Merlin knew that his own time with the king was almost over.",
    "One day, a young lady named Nimue came to Camelot. She was one of the ladies of the Lake, and she was clever and quick. Merlin was fascinated by her. He began to follow her everywhere, and he offered to teach her his magic.",
    "Nimue became Merlin's apprentice. Day after day, she learned his spells. But Nimue was afraid of Merlin. She worried that someday he might use his powerful magic against her. So she listened carefully and learned everything she could, waiting for the day when she would be stronger than he was.",
    "Merlin knew all of this. He could foresee the future, and he saw clearly what Nimue planned to do. Before he left Camelot with her, he went to see King Arthur.",
    "“My lord,” Merlin said, “I must tell you some things before I go. Keep Excalibur's scabbard safe, for someone you trust will try to steal it. And I will not be with you much longer. I will soon be shut away where no one can find me.”",
    "Arthur was alarmed. “You have so much magic,” he said. “If you know this will happen, why don't you stop it?”",
    "Merlin shook his head. “Some things cannot be changed, not even by me,” he said. “It is my fate.”",
    "Merlin traveled with Nimue across the sea and back again, teaching her all the time. At last they came to Cornwall. There, Merlin showed her a wonderful cave beneath a great rock. It was hidden deep in the forest and closed by a huge stone.",
    "Merlin went inside to show Nimue the marvels of the cave. While he was inside, Nimue spoke one of the spells he had taught her. The great stone rolled across the opening, and it sealed the cave shut. No matter how many spells Merlin cast, he could not get out. He had taught her too well.",
    "Merlin was never seen again. Some people say that he is still there, sleeping under the stone, waiting for the day when Britain needs him again.",
    "Back in Camelot, King Arthur missed his old friend and teacher terribly. Without Merlin, Arthur would have to rely on his own wisdom and on the loyalty of his knights. Nimue, who had learned so much from Merlin, did not become Arthur's enemy. In the years ahead, she would use her magic to help the king more than once."])

ch("The Birth of Galahad",
   [("descendant", "a person who comes later in a family; a great-great-grandchild"), ("holy", "sacred; belonging to God"),
    ("grail", "a cup or dish; the Holy Grail was the cup Christ used at the Last Supper"), ("purpose", "the reason for which something is done")],
   ["Many years before Arthur's time, a holy man named Joseph of Arimathea came to Britain. He brought with him a great treasure: the Holy Grail, the cup that Christ had used at the Last Supper. The Grail was kept in a castle called Corbenic, guarded by the descendants of Joseph. In Arthur's time, the keeper of the Grail was King Pelles. King Pelles was a relative of King Pellam, who had been wounded by Balin's Dolorous Stroke.",
    "King Pelles knew an old prophecy. It said that a knight would be born in his family who would be the best knight in the world. This knight would find the Holy Grail, heal the wounded king, and bring life back to the wasteland.",
    "One day, Sir Lancelot came riding near Corbenic. In a nearby town, the people begged him for help. A lady had been trapped in a tower by an enchantment. For five years she had been locked in a room of scalding water. Many knights had tried to save her, but none had been able to break the spell. Lancelot went up to the tower, and when he took the lady by the hand, the enchantment broke. She stepped out free.",
    "Next, the people led Lancelot to a tomb where a fierce dragon lived. Lancelot fought the dragon and slew it.",
    "King Pelles welcomed Lancelot to Corbenic and gave a feast in his honor. During the feast, a beautiful maiden walked through the hall carrying a golden vessel covered with cloth. A wonderful smell filled the room, and every person at the table found that they had the food they liked best. “That,” said King Pelles quietly, “was the Holy Grail.”",
    "King Pelles had a daughter named Elaine. Elaine's lady-in-waiting, who knew magic, cast an enchantment, and because of it, as the prophecy said, Elaine and Lancelot were joined together for a time. A son was born to them, and they named the baby Galahad.",
    "When Lancelot learned that he had been tricked by enchantment, he was upset, and he left Corbenic. But he did not forget the child. Galahad was the son of the greatest knight in the world, and he was a descendant of the keepers of the Holy Grail.",
    "Elaine sent Galahad to be raised by the nuns at a quiet abbey not far from Camelot. There he grew up learning to pray, to work hard, and to be gentle and honest. He learned to ride and to use a sword, but he also learned that a knight's true strength comes from a pure heart.",
    "Galahad grew tall and handsome. He looked very much like his father. But he was different from Lancelot in one important way. Lancelot was a great knight, but he had made mistakes and kept secrets. Galahad was pure. He had no secrets in his heart, and he had never done anything shameful.",
    "Everyone who met the young man felt that he had a special purpose. They did not yet know what it was. But the empty seat at the Round Table was still waiting."])

ch("The Coming of Galahad to Court",
   [("marvel", "something wonderful or amazing"), ("Pentecost", "a church holiday in late spring"),
    ("hush", "a sudden silence"), ("worthy", "good enough; deserving")],
   ["It was the feast of Pentecost, and all the knights of the Round Table had gathered at Camelot. Before the feast began, a squire ran into the hall. “Sir King,” he cried, “come and see a marvel!”",
    "Down at the river, a great block of red marble was floating on the water like a piece of wood. Stuck in it was a beautiful sword with a jeweled handle. Golden letters on the stone said: NO ONE SHALL TAKE ME FROM HERE BUT THE ONE AT WHOSE SIDE I SHALL HANG. HE SHALL BE THE BEST KNIGHT IN THE WORLD.",
    "King Arthur turned to Lancelot. “This sword must be for you,” he said. “You are the best knight in the world.” But Lancelot shook his head. “It is not my sword,” he said. “And I would not dare to touch it.” Sir Gawaine and Sir Percival each tried to pull out the sword, but it would not move.",
    "Everyone went back to the hall and sat down for the feast. Then something strange happened. All the doors and windows shut by themselves. Yet the hall did not grow dark. A soft light filled the room.",
    "In the hush that followed, an old man in a white robe walked in. No one knew how he had entered with the doors shut. With him came a young knight in red armor, with no sword and no shield. The old man said, “Peace be with you, King Arthur. I bring you the young knight who is descended from Joseph of Arimathea. Through him the marvels of this land will be completed.”",
    "The old man led the young knight straight to the Siege Perilous, the dangerous seat that had been empty since the Round Table began. Everyone held their breath. They remembered Merlin's warning: anyone who sat there without being worthy would be destroyed.",
    "The old man lifted a silk cloth from the back of the seat. Golden letters had appeared there. They said: THIS IS THE SEAT OF GALAHAD, THE HIGH PRINCE.",
    "Galahad sat down in the Siege Perilous, and nothing happened to him at all. The knights of the Round Table gasped in amazement. Then they began to whisper. “This is the knight who will find the Holy Grail,” they said to one another. Many of them welcomed Galahad warmly and honored him, because they had never seen anyone sit in that seat and live.",
    "Lancelot looked at the young knight's face and knew at once that this was his son. His heart was full of pride.",
    "After the feast, King Arthur led Galahad to the river and showed him the sword in the floating stone. “Sir,” said Arthur, “some of my best knights have failed to draw this sword.” Galahad answered quietly, “That is no surprise, my lord. This adventure was not meant for them. It was meant for me. I brought no sword with me, because I knew this one was waiting.” He took the handle and drew the sword out easily.",
    "That afternoon there was a tournament. Galahad borrowed a shield and rode against the knights of the Round Table. He defeated every knight he faced except his father, Lancelot, and his friend Percival, whom he did not fight. The whole court agreed: Galahad was the best knight in the world, just as the prophecy had said."])

ch("How the Quest Began",
   [("quest", "a long search for something important"), ("vow", "a solemn promise"),
    ("samite", "a rich silk cloth"), ("grieve", "to feel deep sadness")],
   ["That same evening, the knights sat down to supper in the great hall. Suddenly there was a crack of thunder so loud that it seemed the walls would fall. Then a beam of sunlight, seven times brighter than any daylight, shone into the hall. Every knight looked at his neighbor, and each one seemed more handsome and noble than he had ever looked before. No one could speak a word.",
    "Then the Holy Grail entered the hall. It was covered with white samite, so no one could see it clearly, and no one could see who was carrying it. As it passed through the room, a sweet smell filled the air, and every knight found before him the food and drink he liked best. Then the Grail left the hall as quietly as it had come, and no one knew where it had gone.",
    "When the knights could speak again, Sir Gawaine jumped to his feet. “We have been fed by the Holy Grail,” he said, “but we could not see it because it was covered. I make a vow. Tomorrow I will leave Camelot to search for the Holy Grail. I will not come back for a year and a day, or longer, until I have seen it more clearly than I saw it here.”",
    "As soon as Gawaine had spoken, the other knights jumped up and made the same vow, one after another. Before long, every knight of the Round Table had promised to go on the quest.",
    "King Arthur's face turned pale. “Gawaine,” he said, “you have almost killed me with this vow. You have taken from me the best company of knights the world has ever seen. Once they leave, I know that many of them will never come back. They will die on the quest.” Tears ran down the king's face. “I have loved them as much as my own life,” he said. “Now I must grieve to see them go.”",
    "Queen Guenevere and the ladies of the court wept too. Some of the ladies wanted to go with their knights, but an old hermit warned that the quest was not for them. It was a holy journey, and each knight had to go alone, with a pure heart.",
    "The next morning, Arthur and Guenevere watched from the castle as the knights rode out of Camelot. The streets were filled with people crying and waving goodbye. The knights rode together until they came to a forest. There they agreed to separate, each to follow his own path, because they thought it would be shameful to travel in a group. Each knight entered the forest where it looked thickest and darkest, where there was no path at all.",
    "So began the Quest for the Holy Grail, the greatest adventure the knights of the Round Table would ever undertake. They thought it would be a test of their courage. They would soon discover that it was also a test of their hearts."])

ch("Sir Gawaine on the Quest",
   [("hermit", "a holy person who lives alone to pray"), ("repent", "to feel sorry for wrong things you have done and change"),
    ("stubborn", "refusing to change your mind"), ("vision", "something seen in a dream or by holy power")],
   ["Sir Gawaine was King Arthur's nephew and one of the bravest knights of the Round Table. He was strong and loyal, but he could also be proud and quick-tempered. He was the knight who had first sworn to seek the Grail, and he set out full of confidence.",
    "Gawaine rode for many days, looking for adventures. He fought knights and won tournaments, but he never came any closer to the Grail. He began to feel bored and frustrated.",
    "One day he stopped at the hut of a hermit, an old holy man who lived alone in the forest. The hermit looked at him kindly. “Sir Gawaine,” he said, “you have been a knight for a long time, and you have done many wrong things. You have fought out of pride and killed knights without good reason. The Grail is not found by fighting. If you want to see it, you must first repent of your wrongs. Stay with me a while and set your heart right.”",
    "But Gawaine was stubborn. “I do not have time for that,” he said. “My friends are waiting for me.” And he rode on.",
    "Soon Gawaine met another knight, Sir Ector de Maris, who was Lancelot's brother. Ector, too, was having no luck on the quest. “I have met twenty knights,” said Ector, “and they all say the same thing. No one has found anything.” The two friends rode on together.",
    "One night, they took shelter in an old ruined chapel. Both of them had strange dreams. Gawaine dreamed of a field full of bulls. Most of the bulls were proud and spotted, but three were pure white. The spotted bulls left the field to look for better grass, and many of them never returned. Ector dreamed that he and Lancelot came to a house but were turned away.",
    "In the morning, they went to a holy man to ask what the dreams meant. “The bulls are the knights of the Round Table,” he told Gawaine. “The spotted ones are the knights whose hearts are stained by pride and wrongdoing. They will not find the Grail. The three white bulls are three knights who are pure. They are the ones who will succeed.” Gawaine did not like this answer.",
    "Later, Gawaine and Ector met a knight in the forest who challenged them to a joust. Gawaine charged and struck the knight so hard that his spear went through the knight's shield and armor. The knight fell to the ground. When Gawaine lifted the knight's helmet, he cried out in horror. It was Sir Uwaine, his own cousin and a knight of the Round Table. Because they wore unfamiliar shields, they had not known each other. Uwaine forgave Gawaine, and then he died.",
    "Gawaine was heartbroken. He realized that the hermit had been right. He had been fighting without thinking, and now he had killed his own kinsman.",
    "Gawaine finally understood that the Grail quest was not for him. The adventures he was used to, the jousts and battles, were not the way to the Grail. He and Ector turned their horses around and slowly rode back toward Camelot. The quest would be won by knights whose hearts were purer than his."])
